00:42:06.450 --> 00:42:07.850
and now I'm breaking state records.

1026
00:42:07.850 --> 00:42:09.753
And the momentum just kind of flows.

1027
00:42:09.753 --> 00:42:14.753
Right, so at 10, what was the epiphany

1028
00:42:15.649 --> 00:42:17.210
in terms of the Olympics?

1029
00:42:17.210 --> 00:42:19.010
Like, was there something on television

1030
00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:20.833
or was there somebody you were looking up to

1031
00:42:20.833 --> 00:42:23.634
that, like that light bulb went off?

1032
00:42:23.634 --> 00:42:27.660
2000, I had just turned 11.

1033
00:42:27.660 --> 00:42:31.116
And that was when I remember us we were on vacation

1034
00:42:31.116 --> 00:42:33.454
at the beach and we had the Olympics on.

1035
00:42:33.454 --> 00:42:36.930
And I remember Kaitlin Sandeno was 17,

1036
00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:38.750
and she was swimming 400 IM.

1037
00:42:38.750 --> 00:42:42.110
And she was, I think she, she meddled in a couple of events

1038
00:42:42.110 --> 00:42:46.010
and I was like wow, like, how cool is she.

1039
00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:49.450
She has this cap, you know, USA cap with her name on it.

1040
00:42:49.450 --> 00:42:52.140
Like I just was enamored by the whole thing

1041
00:42:52.140 --> 00:42:55.880
and not having any idea of how do you make an Olympic team?

1042
00:42:55.880 --> 00:42:58.100
You know, how how'd she get there, what did she do?

1043
00:42:58.100 --> 00:43:00.810
I just was like, oh, I need to do that.

1044
00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:01.980
Yeah.

1045
00:43:01.980 --> 00:43:04.460
And of course I think, you know every, every kid

1046
00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:06.730
at that age says like, I want to be an Olympian, you know?

1047
00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:08.430
And so I remember always being.

1048
00:43:08.430 --> 00:43:09.650
I don't think they do though?

1049
00:43:09.650 --> 00:43:10.861
You don't think so?

1050
00:43:10.861 --> 00:43:12.021
No, I don't think so.

1051
00:43:12.021 --> 00:43:13.640
I think a lot of kids are just having fun

1052
00:43:13.640 --> 00:43:15.875
or the parents are like you need to get out of the house.

1053
00:43:15.875 --> 00:43:17.190
And not like, all right.

1054
00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:18.906
What's what's the time you gotta make it.

1055
00:43:18.906 --> 00:43:22.560
Yeah, I guess maybe a little older, but I think

1056
00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:24.900
I was saying it with like intent, like I was saying like

1057
00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:27.820
okay, like, all right, so what's the next cut?

1058
00:43:27.820 --> 00:43:30.922
What's the trials cut, you know what I need to do, and.

1059
00:43:30.922 --> 00:43:32.993
A tenure, like what's the trials cut.

1060
00:43:32.993 --> 00:43:36.041
(Katie and Rich laughs)

1061
00:43:36.041 --> 00:43:37.907
Your parents must have been like,

1062
00:43:37.907 --> 00:43:39.530
"What is going on with these kid?"

1063
00:43:39.530 --> 00:43:41.740
They definitely had to hold me back a little bit,

1064
00:43:41.740 --> 00:43:46.120
because I remember at 11 I was angry

1065
00:43:46.120 --> 00:43:47.730
as all get out because the older kids

1066
00:43:47.730 --> 00:43:49.560
were doing doubles two a days.

1067
00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:51.793
And I was like, well, I need to be doing two a days.

1068
00:43:51.793 --> 00:43:53.681
Like how can I be doing two a day?

1069
00:43:53.681 --> 00:43:55.540
And they were like, you have plenty of time in your life

1070
00:43:55.540 --> 00:43:56.560
to be doing doubles.

1071
00:43:56.560 --> 00:43:59.408
And so they held me back a little bit, but I mean,

1072
00:43:59.408 --> 00:44:04.408
was pretty hard once I was released, I was locked in.

1073
00:44:04.450 --> 00:44:09.450
And yeah, I remember I made my first nationals at 12 and.

1074
00:44:10.670 --> 00:44:11.800
That's crazy.

1075
00:44:11.800 --> 00:44:14.358
Yeah, and just I mean, I didn't swim well, but I.

1076
00:44:14.358 --> 00:44:17.200
You must have been the youngest person by a long shot.

1077
00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:19.470
Yeah, I think,

1078
00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:21.380
yeah I think at that national, is that the only reason.

1079
00:44:21.380 --> 00:44:25.710
You see young like, like boys don't end up qualifying

1080
00:44:25.710 --> 00:44:28.890
for nationals until like the very early, like 16

1081
00:44:28.890 --> 00:44:31.727
maybe a 15 year old women, it tends to happen

1082
00:44:31.727 --> 00:44:32.930
a little bit younger.

1083
00:44:32.930 --> 00:44:34.480
There's the occasional 13 year old

1084
00:44:34.480 --> 00:44:37.100
but I've never heard of a 12 year old making nationals.

1085
00:44:37.100 --> 00:44:38.990
I think the only other, and there's been,

1086
00:44:38.990 --> 00:44:41.320
yeah it's like very spotty throughout the years.

1087
00:44:41.320 --> 00:44:42.810
You know, Dana Vollmer.

1088
00:44:42.810 --> 00:44:45.930
I remember she was at trials at 12 or something like that,

1089
00:44:45.930 --> 00:44:49.060
but yeah, it was, again it was rare,

1090
00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:50.933
but in that instance, I just didn't.

1091
00:44:50.933 --> 00:44:52.487
You were like, of course.

1092
00:44:52.487 --> 00:44:54.750
I'm like this is normal and it was kind of good.

1093
00:44:54.750 --> 00:44:57.296
I think, to be honest that I didn't,

1094
00:44:57.296 --> 00:44:59.210
I didn't feel no one made me feel like I was abnormal

1095
00:44:59.210 --> 00:45:02.270
or if they did, if they were like what's,

1096
00:45:02.270 --> 00:45:03.607
I found, I felt special.

1097
00:45:03.607 --> 00:45:05.360
You know, I felt excited.

1098
00:45:05.360 --> 00:45:07.370
Like, wow, you know, it must I must be doing something

1099
00:45:07.370 --> 00:45:08.670
pretty cool here.

1100
00:45:08.670 --> 00:45:11.218
And I remember the boys that I swam with

1101
00:45:11.218 --> 00:45:13.840
'cause I saw him at 15, 16 year old boys.

1102
00:45:13.840 --> 00:45:16.980
And boys I say they were definitely not men,

1103
00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:20.436
boys were jealous or you know,

1104
00:45:20.436 --> 00:45:22.350
and that may maybe even more like,

1105
00:45:22.350 --> 00:45:24.763
"Oh, like I'm just gonna be competitive about this."

1106
00:45:24.763 --> 00:45:27.360
But at that me in 2002 I watched Natalie Coughlin

1107
00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:29.500
break the world record in the a hundred backstroke,

1108
00:45:29.500 --> 00:45:31.080
and go under a minute for the first time.

1109
00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:33.700
And I remember that even more.

1110
00:45:33.700 --> 00:45:36.742
So I was like, oh, like Olympics, but like,

1111
00:45:36.742 --> 00:45:38.660
now there's a world record on the table.

1112
00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:41.587
Like that just kind of upped the ante for me in terms of

1113
00:45:41.587 --> 00:45:44.730
really lighting the fire of, okay.

1114
00:45:44.730 --> 00:45:47.430
I need to do all of these things now, how do I do it.

1115
00:45:47.430 --> 00:45:51.294
Yeah, and at 15 you materialize all of this.

1116
00:45:51.294 --> 00:45:54.948
You make your first Olympic team in 2004.

1117
00:45:54.948 --> 00:45:59.910
Yeah, which I even a year out I did not expect that,

1118
00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:02.040
you know, I obviously had the goal to make an Olympic team,

1119
00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:04.349
but I remember sitting down a year out.

1120
00:46:04.349 --> 00:46:07.169
My parents, we moved to Baltimore.

1121
00:46:07.169 --> 00:46:09.892
Yeah, a year out from 2004.

1122
00:46:09.892 --> 00:46:12.760
Was that specifically to join NBAC

1123
00:46:12.760 --> 00:46:14.533
or your dad got a job or how did that work?

1124
00:46:14.533 --> 00:46:16.292
Half and half.

1125
00:46:16.292 --> 00:46:17.390
Yeah.
Half and half,

1126
00:46:17.390 --> 00:46:19.820
at that time I was doing well on my team in Virginia,

1127
00:46:19.820 --> 00:46:21.550
but it was it was tough because again,

1128
00:46:21.550 --> 00:46:23.810
I was swimming with boys and I didn't really

1129
00:46:23.810 --> 00:46:25.520
have any female training partners

1130
00:46:25.520 --> 00:46:29.590
and I just felt like we needed something more.

1131
00:46:29.590 --> 00:46:31.560
And at the time too my dad was commuting

1132
00:46:31.560 --> 00:46:32.560
to Maryland anyways.

1133
00:46:32.560 --> 00:46:34.730
So we were like, let's give it a shot.

1134
00:46:34.730 --> 00:46:37.057
And I clicked really well with with Paul Yetter

1135
00:46:37.057 --> 00:46:41.350
who, you know gave me exactly what my robot self wanted

1136
00:46:41.350 --> 00:46:43.037
in terms of times.

1137
00:46:43.037 --> 00:46:45.730
And I remember we drove up there for

1138
00:46:45.730 --> 00:46:48.366
like a kind of a trial practice on a Saturday morning.

1139
00:46:48.366 --> 00:46:51.410
And we were doing a set of two hundreds,

1140
00:46:51.410 --> 00:46:53.320
and he showed me the piece of paper

1141
00:46:53.320 --> 00:46:56.540
and it was literal splits for each 200 and my eyes lit up.

1142
00:46:56.540 --> 00:46:58.390
Was like, this is my guy.

1143
00:46:58.390 --> 00:47:00.790
I have like, we are moving.

1144
00:47:00.790 --> 00:47:02.440
You got me, I'm sold so.

1145
00:47:02.440 --> 00:47:04.250
Yeah, what was interesting about

1146
00:47:04.250 --> 00:47:06.139
about reading about that in your book,

1147
00:47:06.139 --> 00:47:09.830
was that, I guess I just assumed that if you're swimming

1148
00:47:09.830 --> 00:47:11.020
at NBA, see you're swimming

1149
00:47:11.020 --> 00:47:13.780
for Bob Bowman and there's that one pool, right.

1150
00:47:13.780 --> 00:47:15.565
And I didn't realize that it was more like

1151
00:47:15.565 --> 00:47:18.590
the team that I swam for curl where there's multiple pools

1152
00:47:18.590 --> 00:47:20.790
around the region and there's lots of different coaches

1153
00:47:20.790 --> 00:47:22.540
and you were swimming in this

1154
00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:24.479
really shitty tiny little pool,

1155
00:47:24.479 --> 00:47:27.005
like four lanes and like, yeah.

1156
00:47:27.005 --> 00:47:28.420
It was called Night Divers.

1157
00:47:28.420 --> 00:47:29.253
So yeah, there was like.

1158
00:47:29.253 --> 00:47:31.007
It was like a scuba diving tank or something?

1159
00:47:31.007 --> 00:47:34.186
Pretty much it had this bubble over it.

1160
00:47:34.186 --> 00:47:37.140
And the, I mean the, I was coughing every night.

1161
00:47:37.140 --> 00:47:39.470
Like it was, you didn't roll up to this place

1162
00:47:39.470 --> 00:47:41.210
with this bubble and think an Olympian

1163
00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:43.300
is gonna come from this pool.

1164
00:47:43.300 --> 00:47:44.320
Right.

1165
00:47:44.320 --> 00:47:46.786
It was hot, but yeah, it was a group of 10 or 12 of us

1166
00:47:46.786 --> 00:47:50.107
and the, the girl Courtney Kailash

1167
00:47:50.107 --> 00:47:52.543
her brother chase is actually an Olympian now.

1168
00:47:52.543 --> 00:47:56.920
We had met at a regional meet and hit it off really well.

1169
00:47:56.920 --> 00:47:58.988
And you know, she was swimming really fast

1170
00:47:58.988 --> 00:48:02.184
and I I felt like, okay, great.

1171
00:48:02.184 --> 00:48:03.740
Like I can have a female training partner.

1172
00:48:03.740 --> 00:48:06.207
And so that was kind of the draw as well as Paul was having,

1173
00:48:06.207 --> 00:48:09.050
you know, a girl that I could I could connect with

1174
00:48:09.050 --> 00:48:09.883
and train with.

1175
00:48:09.883 --> 00:48:11.762
And we became really good friends.

1176
00:48:11.762 --> 00:48:15.365
So you go to trials, you qualify in two events,

1177
00:48:15.365 --> 00:48:17.410
200 IM and 400 IM, right.

1178
00:48:17.410 --> 00:48:20.109
You're only 15 years old.

1179
00:48:20.109 --> 00:48:22.520
That had to be, you know.

1180
00:48:22.520 --> 00:48:23.353
Surreal.

1181
00:48:23.353 --> 00:48:24.682
Yeah, very surreal.

1182
00:48:24.682 --> 00:48:26.920
And then you're faced with the prospect

1183
00:48:26.920 --> 00:48:28.640
of actually having to do it at the Olympics.

1184
00:48:28.640 --> 00:48:29.750
I think people don't understand

1185
00:48:29.750 --> 00:48:31.360
like it's all about trials, right.

1186
00:48:31.360 --> 00:48:33.870
Like your, your perspective doesn't really extend

1187
00:48:33.870 --> 00:48:36.110
beyond that because it's all about making the Olympic team.

1188
00:48:36.110 --> 00:48:37.870
Then you make it, then you're like,

1189
00:48:37.870 --> 00:48:39.500
oh shit, I've got to actually go

1190
00:48:39.500 --> 00:48:41.283
to the Olympics now and swim.

1191
00:48:41.283 --> 00:48:43.770
But you were so young, I can't imagine

1192
00:48:43.770 --> 00:48:45.990
that there was a huge amount of expectation

1193
00:48:45.990 --> 00:48:50.240
and pressure on you to like metal or win gold.

1194
00:48:50.240 --> 00:48:53.140
You know, like win gold or anything like that,

1195
00:48:53.140 --> 00:48:55.563
maybe on yourself, but externally.

1196
00:48:56.440 --> 00:48:59.920
I feel like I'm, I'm very thankful that social media

1197
00:48:59.920 --> 00:49:02.173
wasn't as big back then what 17 years ago.

1198
00:49:02.173 --> 00:49:04.497
But I remember reading articles and,

1199
00:49:04.497 --> 00:49:08.090
you know the day after Charles was done,

1200
00:49:08.090 --> 00:49:10.940
I was with this group of Tom Malchow

1201
00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:13.252
and Kaitlin Sandeno who I just watched four years earlier

1202
00:49:13.252 --> 00:49:16.468
to be in this big sports illustrated thing.

1203
00:49:16.468 --> 00:49:18.470
So there was that, there was all of these,

1204
00:49:18.470 --> 00:49:20.470
there was articles about meddling.

1205
00:49:20.470 --> 00:49:23.606
So I felt like, at least at the time it felt like,

1206
00:49:23.606 --> 00:49:27.570
okay, like now you're expected to win medals

1207
00:49:27.570 --> 00:49:30.289
for the US and maybe win gold.

1208
00:49:30.289 --> 00:49:33.850
And, but you're so right.

1209
00:49:33.850 --> 00:49:37.869
I was, so, the year out I was obsessed with just making

1210
00:49:37.869 --> 00:49:39.960
top eight, that's all I wanted to do.

1211
00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:41.140
I was like, you need to make top eight.

1212
00:49:41.140 --> 00:49:44.079
So that in 2008, I'm prepared with the experience.

1213
00:49:44.079 --> 00:49:46.732
Once again, like very I'm prepared for the experience

1214
00:49:46.732 --> 00:49:49.077
of swimming and Olympic trials final, and then,

1215
00:49:49.077 --> 00:49:50.850
you know drop a ton of time.

1216
00:49:50.850 --> 00:49:51.852
I win both events.

1217
00:49:51.852 --> 00:49:56.041
And then yeah, it was like, oh my God, that means

1218
00:49:56.041 --> 00:49:59.920
I have to go swim Olympics and do this all over again.

1219
00:49:59.920 --> 00:50:02.611
And you know, be away from my coach who I clung to.

1220
00:50:02.611 --> 00:50:05.406
You know, he wasn't able to come and be,

1221
00:50:05.406 --> 00:50:08.040
you know on the staff or anything like that.

1222
00:50:08.040 --> 00:50:10.180
And then be away from my parents for six weeks

1223
00:50:10.180 --> 00:50:12.520
which I'd never even been out of the country.

1224
00:50:12.520 --> 00:50:14.820
So I was, I was just turned 15 year old,

1225
00:50:14.820 --> 00:50:16.753
but I was a very naive,

1226
00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:22.210
looked like I was 12, 15 year old.

1227
00:50:22.210 --> 00:50:25.413
Just wasn't, just was in over my head.

1228
00:50:25.413 --> 00:50:28.237
Yeah, one of the things you talk about in your book is,

1229
00:50:28.237 --> 00:50:33.237
is like not exactly getting the kind of support

1230
00:50:33.290 --> 00:50:36.792
that you wish that you could have had as a young person.

1231
00:50:36.792 --> 00:50:39.690
Yeah, you know, and it's funny

1232
00:50:39.690 --> 00:50:41.890
I've talked to so many people at USA swimming,

1233
00:50:41.890 --> 00:50:45.700
now and they've been so great about saying like,

1234
00:50:45.700 --> 00:50:47.520
yeah like things are so different now

1235
00:50:47.520 --> 00:50:49.477
in the way that we support,

1236
00:50:49.477 --> 00:50:51.530
you know newcomers on the team.

1237
00:50:51.530 --> 00:50:54.734
Or even the, the building of the world junior team,

1238
00:50:54.734 --> 00:50:58.211
where it allows young people to go and travel the world as,

1239
00:50:58.211 --> 00:51:01.520
as a national junior teamer.

1240
00:51:01.520 --> 00:51:03.690
And they have that when I was at that age

1241
00:51:03.690 --> 00:51:05.020
but it wasn't as prolific.

1242
00:51:05.020 --> 00:51:06.160
It wasn't as,

1243
00:51:06.160 --> 00:51:09.640
hey you need to do this because those things matter

1244
00:51:09.640 --> 00:51:12.320
that experience helps you feel confident when you're

1245
00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:14.180
in a ready room with all these different people

1246
00:51:14.180 --> 00:51:16.170
from all these different countries.

1247
00:51:16.170 --> 00:51:17.520
To me, it felt like the hunger games

1248
00:51:17.520 --> 00:51:19.583
it just felt like happening.

1249
00:51:20.430 --> 00:51:22.430
But I think another thing is

1250
00:51:23.390 --> 00:51:26.900
I never now looking back I don't really expect it,

1251
00:51:26.900 --> 00:51:31.900
because everyone is facing their own struggles

1252
00:51:32.249 --> 00:51:35.980
coaches, athletes, and just the Olympics, it's the ultimate.

1253
00:51:35.980 --> 00:51:38.582
And so I think it's very difficult to

1254
00:51:38.582 --> 00:51:41.621
step outside of yourself and see

1255
00:51:41.621 --> 00:51:43.414
that someone else is struggling.

1256
00:51:43.414 --> 00:51:45.440
And there are people that did it.

1257
00:51:45.440 --> 00:51:48.633
And I shouted them out, Gary Hall Jr, Jason Lee's Zach.

1258
00:51:50.157 --> 00:51:52.110
To do that in that pressure cooker

1259
00:51:52.110 --> 00:51:57.011
that is the Olympic games, I think it's above and beyond.

1260
00:51:57.011 --> 00:52:01.668
And I, yeah, I do wish that maybe I'd had

1261
00:52:01.668 --> 00:52:05.180
someone understand what was going on and, and intervene.

1262
00:52:05.180 --> 00:52:08.046
Or if I had felt confident to say,

1263
00:52:08.046 --> 00:52:09.920
hey, I need some support.

1264
00:52:09.920 --> 00:52:11.450
I'm kind of floundering here,

1265
00:52:11.450 --> 00:52:13.360
but I just, I just don't think that

1266
00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:14.430
that was there at the time.

1267
00:52:14.430 --> 00:52:18.800
And I think that everyone was just trying to forge ahead.

1268
00:52:18.800 --> 00:52:19.670
Yeah, well, they're always thinking

1269
00:52:19.670 --> 00:52:20.880
about their own shit.

1270
00:52:20.880 --> 00:52:23.650
You know like they've got their own races to worry about,

1271
00:52:23.650 --> 00:52:26.869
do you, do you know who Alexi Pappas is?

1272
00:52:26.869 --> 00:52:28.440
Yeah, she was a guest.

1273
00:52:28.440 --> 00:52:29.300
Yeah, she was a guest.

1274
00:52:29.300 --> 00:52:32.967
So she is an Olympian track and field athlete

1275
00:52:32.967 --> 00:52:36.900
competed for Greece in the 10,000 meters, I think.

1276
00:52:36.900 --> 00:52:41.900
But she's also an actress, a writer, director producer.

1277
00:52:42.896 --> 00:52:47.480
And in the, and she made this film called Olympic Dreams.

1278
00:52:47.480 --> 00:52:50.139
That's a fictionalized account of

1279
00:52:50.139 --> 00:52:55.139
not really her story, but the story of a young female

1280
00:52:55.430 --> 00:52:57.520
athlete who goes to the winter Olympic games

1281
00:52:57.520 --> 00:52:59.185
as a cross country skier.

1282
00:52:59.185 --> 00:53:03.351
And has kind of a, not so great experience like,

1283
00:53:03.351 --> 00:53:07.260
and the emotions of like contending with that

1284
00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:10.440
where her coach is not there and she feels very alone.

1285
00:53:10.440 --> 00:53:11.980
And I just, when you're recounting that story

1286
00:53:11.980 --> 00:53:14.543
I was like, oh, you gotta watch this movie.

1287
00:53:14.543 --> 00:53:17.770
Yeah, I'm sure I can relate to it very much so.

1288
00:53:17.770 --> 00:53:22.420
Yeah looking back and even, I think being outside of it

1289
00:53:22.420 --> 00:53:24.530
you have conversations with people that make you go,

1290
00:53:24.530 --> 00:53:27.250
oh wow, how, how did I do that, you know.

1291
00:53:27.250 --> 00:53:30.271
Like people talk about like what they were doing at 15.

1292
00:53:30.271 --> 00:53:34.388
I'm about to walk out in a final or for the Olympics.

1293
00:53:34.388 --> 00:53:37.300
But in that moment, you're just it's fight or flight.

1294
00:53:37.300 --> 00:53:40.013
Like, am I gonna run or am I gonna face this, and

1295
00:53:40.013 --> 00:53:43.467
I just remember just terror, just full on terror,

1296
00:53:43.467 --> 00:53:45.792
just sitting there and like,

1297
00:53:45.792 --> 00:53:48.890
I used to do this thing where people would

1298
00:53:48.890 --> 00:53:50.070
if someone was walking by,

1299
00:53:50.070 --> 00:53:51.920
like I'd look at them and be like

1300
00:53:51.920 --> 00:53:53.550
I kind of wish I was like, just could trade places

1301
00:53:53.550 --> 00:53:55.900
with that person for just like, just like 10 minutes.

1302
00:53:55.900 --> 00:53:57.900
Like just for this race to be done.

1303
00:53:57.900 --> 00:53:59.210
Yeah, but you don't know what's going on

1304
00:53:59.210 --> 00:54:00.290
in that person's mind.

1305
00:54:00.290 --> 00:54:01.917
It could be the same thing,

1306
00:54:01.917 --> 00:54:03.978
it could be a nightmare inside, inside their head.

1307
00:54:03.978 --> 00:54:07.364
So not a good game, but that was just how much I

1308
00:54:07.364 --> 00:54:09.600
in that moment wanting to flee.

1309
00:54:09.600 --> 00:54:13.420
Like, it was just, I didn't I hadn't kind of developed

1310
00:54:13.420 --> 00:54:16.493
that system of confidence, and I feared the 400 IM

1311
00:54:17.354 --> 00:54:19.000
for the longest time.

1312
00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:23.085
Because I just felt like I had messed up so many times

1313
00:54:23.085 --> 00:54:25.280
or taking it out too fast and died.

1314
00:54:25.280 --> 00:54:26.690
And there was just like this,

1315
00:54:26.690 --> 00:54:29.967
I viewed it as like this mountain that just kept beating me

1316
00:54:29.967 --> 00:54:32.363
and I could never get back to the top.

1317
00:54:32.363 --> 00:54:34.520
Yeah, you don't end up meddling.

1318
00:54:34.520 --> 00:54:38.853
You get seventh in the 200 IM in 17th in the 400, right.

1319
00:54:39.830 --> 00:54:41.120
Throw up all over the pool deck.

1320
00:54:41.120 --> 00:54:45.103
What's that throwing up, that's okay, you know.

1321
00:54:46.910 --> 00:54:49.330
But the following year, I mean, look

1322
00:54:49.330 --> 00:54:51.520
you know Michael Phelps went to the Olympics at 15

1323
00:54:51.520 --> 00:54:53.132
and didn't mettle.

1324
00:54:53.132 --> 00:54:53.990
You know, people forget that.

1325
00:54:53.990 --> 00:54:56.350
Like they just think it's all goals all the time.

1326
00:54:56.350 --> 00:54:58.150
But when at his first Olympics,

1327
00:54:58.150 --> 00:55:00.210
you know I remember when that happened

1328
00:55:00.210 --> 00:55:01.878
and everyone's like, oh he's the future.

1329
00:55:01.878 --> 00:55:05.144
But it's not like he won a gold medal first time out.

1330
00:55:05.144 --> 00:55:07.980
So you go home and the following year,

1331
00:55:07.980 --> 00:55:11.320
you sign this like 10 year deal with Speedo and you go pro.

1332
00:55:11.320 --> 00:55:14.415
And suddenly you're buying cars and like handbags and stuff.

1333
00:55:14.415 --> 00:55:16.316
(Katie laughs)

1334
00:55:16.316 --> 00:55:18.143
And, and like, you know, it's all good, right.

1335
00:55:18.143 --> 00:55:19.497
The world is your oyster

1336
00:55:19.497 --> 00:55:23.758
because it's all gonna be about the next Olympics.

1337
00:55:23.758 --> 00:55:28.013
Yeah, yeah that was a, a crazy year

1338
00:55:28.013 --> 00:55:30.674
because, you know, I came home from Athens

1339
00:55:30.674 --> 00:55:32.633
kind of licked my wounds a little bit.

1340
00:55:32.633 --> 00:55:34.950
But there was never a feeling of like,

1341
00:55:34.950 --> 00:55:38.290
I'm done or I don't know if I can do this.

1342
00:55:38.290 --> 00:55:40.622
It was just redemption.

1343
00:55:40.622 --> 00:55:43.290
I was like, I want to show people that,

1344
00:55:43.290 --> 00:55:47.200
that wasn't a fluke that, you know just.

1345
00:55:47.200 --> 00:55:49.064
But it's interesting you use the word redemption.

1346
00:55:49.064 --> 00:55:51.239
Like what did you feel like you had to redeem,

1347
00:55:51.239 --> 00:55:54.191
because you didn't metal, like you were 15?

1348
00:55:54.191 --> 00:55:56.195
But that was for me,

1349
00:55:56.195 --> 00:56:00.510
that was, I had, I had flat out failed.

1350
00:56:00.510 --> 00:56:03.250
Like the, the art, the articles that were coming out

1351
00:56:03.250 --> 00:56:05.558
that I shouldn't have read.

1352
00:56:05.558 --> 00:56:09.664
I remember specifically, actually someone else told me this

1353
00:56:09.664 --> 00:56:11.680
'cause I didn't fully, I think I blocked it out.

1354
00:56:11.680 --> 00:56:14.356
But all the computers were in the basement, in the village.

1355
00:56:14.356 --> 00:56:18.880
And after the 400 IM masochistically go on and,

1356
00:56:18.880 --> 00:56:20.670
and look on the internet.

1357
00:56:20.670 --> 00:56:22.880
And I saw just this flurry of just negativity,

1358
00:56:22.880 --> 00:56:25.200
how I failed, and I fell on my face.

1359
00:56:25.200 --> 00:56:27.578
And just kind of lost it and burst into tears.

1360
00:56:27.578 --> 00:56:31.286
From like no name people or like rowdy and stuff.

1361
00:56:31.286 --> 00:56:33.270
From there, yeah.

1362
00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:35.350
There was a couple, there was a couple of people

1363
00:56:35.350 --> 00:56:37.600
that I, you know, very much looked up to

1364
00:56:37.600 --> 00:56:40.420
that were saying really, really hurtful things.

1365
00:56:40.420 --> 00:56:45.420
And that just, I'm so happy the social media was not

1366
00:56:45.660 --> 00:56:49.170
that way in 2004 because I don't know if I,

1367
00:56:49.170 --> 00:56:51.754
I think it would have been a lot harder to overcome.

1368
00:56:51.754 --> 00:56:53.391
You'd be in your dorm room scrolling.

1369
00:56:53.391 --> 00:56:54.503
Just look yeah.

1370
00:56:54.503 --> 00:56:57.080
And, and I think, you know, for the longest time,

1371
00:56:57.080 --> 00:56:59.300
the picture of me throwing up on the pool deck

1372
00:56:59.300 --> 00:57:02.250
at 15 years old was the first thing that popped up on images

1373
00:57:02.250 --> 00:57:03.770
when you searched my name.

1374
00:57:03.770 --> 00:57:05.750
And my parents had to like ask them to take it down,

1375
00:57:05.750 --> 00:57:07.738
you know at 15, that's.

1376
00:57:07.738 --> 00:57:09.824
Now 15 year olds are like well you know,

1377
00:57:09.824 --> 00:57:12.492
my hair is messed up in that one picture edited, right.

1378
00:57:12.492 --> 00:57:15.723
This was me just, so that was very traumatizing.

1379
00:57:15.723 --> 00:57:20.468
But yeah, I viewed it as I've let everybody down.

1380
00:57:20.468 --> 00:57:24.199
Like we had a party, I remember a welcome home party

1381
00:57:24.199 --> 00:57:25.630
when I got back and I was miserable.

1382
00:57:25.630 --> 00:57:28.990
I don't think people knew, but I didn't feel deserving.

1383
00:57:28.990 --> 00:57:31.460
I was like, which I know it's like,

1384
00:57:31.460 --> 00:57:34.850
when I say it it's crazy, but I was like, I failed.

1385
00:57:34.850 --> 00:57:36.963
Like, I didn't matter.

1386
00:57:37.892 --> 00:57:39.660
Even on seventh in the world at 15,

1387
00:57:39.660 --> 00:57:41.450
why are these people here?

1388
00:57:41.450 --> 00:57:42.768
Like everyone should just leave.

1389
00:57:42.768 --> 00:57:44.470
Like I need to get back to work and.

1390
00:57:44.470 --> 00:57:45.464
Yeah.

1391
00:57:45.464 --> 00:57:46.430
Redeem myself.

1392
00:57:46.430 --> 00:57:47.720
That's, you know and maybe that was.

1393
00:57:47.720 --> 00:57:49.720
And also the compartmentalization too.

1394
00:57:49.720 --> 00:57:52.250
Like that's in the past, I'm putting that away.

1395
00:57:52.250 --> 00:57:53.240
I'm moving forward.

1396
00:57:53.240 --> 00:57:54.960
Here's what I have in front of me to do.

1397
00:57:54.960 --> 00:57:59.710
Yeah, yeah and again, it worked for the time being,

1398
00:57:59.710 --> 00:58:01.773
it was, you know my coach Paul and I sat down

1399
00:58:01.773 --> 00:58:03.860
and it was like, all right,

1400
00:58:03.860 --> 00:58:06.220
well, a, you need to figure out how to not be fearful

1401
00:58:06.220 --> 00:58:10.050
of the 400 IM because it's truly messing with you.

1402
00:58:10.050 --> 00:58:11.922
Yeah, and that is terrifying.

1403
00:58:11.922 --> 00:58:13.656
(Katie and Rich laughs)

1404
00:58:13.656 --> 00:58:14.870
It's the most painful..

1405
00:58:14.870 --> 00:58:16.451
Like no one is in the ready room,

1406
00:58:16.451 --> 00:58:18.790
I have a joke with Elizabeth Beisel

1407
00:58:18.790 --> 00:58:21.750
who's in fellow Olympian 400 IM, amazing.

1408
00:58:21.750 --> 00:58:23.188
Got the silver in 2012.

1409
00:58:23.188 --> 00:58:26.580
And or we call it like the 400 IM stomach.

1410
00:58:26.580 --> 00:58:30.870
Like, it's just a very specific feeling before

1411
00:58:30.870 --> 00:58:33.240
and after that, if you were plopped

1412
00:58:33.240 --> 00:58:35.240
into a situation not having done the 400 IM

1413
00:58:35.240 --> 00:58:36.720
and someone was like, what's this feeling?

1414
00:58:36.720 --> 00:58:37.620
You'd be like, oh.

1415
00:58:38.620 --> 00:58:41.525
Somebody is gonna turn your insides out completely.

1416
00:58:41.525 --> 00:58:42.530
It's just, it's never gonna be,

1417
00:58:42.530 --> 00:58:45.340
whether it's an amazing race, a terrible race,

1418
00:58:45.340 --> 00:58:47.251
you gained 50 seconds you dropped 20,

1419
00:58:47.251 --> 00:58:49.602
is always gonna be the most painful thing ever.

1420
00:58:49.602 --> 00:58:51.910
And I actually have come up

1421
00:58:51.910 --> 00:58:55.460
and talking to one of our executives at my old company.

1422
00:58:55.460 --> 00:58:57.661
And I was like, yeah, it's the 400 IM analogy.

1423
00:58:57.661 --> 00:59:00.060
I was like, are you talking about like,

1424
00:59:00.060 --> 00:59:05.060
well you don't do the 400 IM because like,

1425
00:59:05.175 --> 00:59:08.400
if you, if you pretend in the, and the 400 IM

1426
00:59:08.400 --> 00:59:10.832
that you're like, yes I'm so excited as in this race.

1427
00:59:10.832 --> 00:59:13.332
If you're just, you're excited for the result.

1428
00:59:13.332 --> 00:59:15.197
But like, you're not,

1429
00:59:15.197 --> 00:59:19.866
if you pretend it's not there just pushing it down and,

1430
00:59:19.866 --> 00:59:23.272
and ignoring is almost more emotional energy

1431
00:59:23.272 --> 00:59:25.810
than just saying, wow, this is gonna suck

1432
00:59:25.810 --> 00:59:27.061
and this is super painful.

1433
00:59:27.061 --> 00:59:29.083
And that doesn't mean I'm being negative

1434
00:59:29.083 --> 00:59:32.300
because I'm doing this 'cause I'm trying to break records

1435
00:59:32.300 --> 00:59:33.350
and go best times.

1436
00:59:33.350 --> 00:59:37.190
But by to me, by embracing that in anything

1437
00:59:37.190 --> 00:59:38.577
and acknowledging it,

1438
00:59:38.577 --> 00:59:41.810
it makes it that much easier to attack and go after.

1439
00:59:41.810 --> 00:59:44.840
Right, but what you just said is like a corollary for,

1440
00:59:44.840 --> 00:59:47.460
for your life at the same time, right.

1441
00:59:47.460 --> 00:59:50.880
The idea of ignoring the difficulty

1442
00:59:50.880 --> 00:59:54.502
or the pain of the 400 IM is almost a stand in for

1443
00:59:54.502 --> 00:59:59.502
like that idea of how ultimately compartmentalizing

1444
01:00:00.370 --> 01:00:01.610
these things doesn't work out, right.

1445
01:00:01.610 --> 01:00:04.032
You have to like acknowledge, right.

1446
01:00:04.032 --> 01:00:04.870
(Katie laughs)

1447
01:00:04.870 --> 01:00:06.530
You're saying I just don't take my own advice.

1448
01:00:06.530 --> 01:00:07.410
No, I'm just saying like,

1449
01:00:07.410 --> 01:00:10.010
no, what I'm saying I think you're doing it now

1450
01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:12.520
but what you just said in terms of the 400 IM

1451
01:00:12.520 --> 01:00:13.990
is kind of what we're talking about

1452
01:00:13.990 --> 01:00:15.310
in terms of life as well.

1453
01:00:15.310 --> 01:00:16.143
Oh, for sure.

1454
01:00:16.143 --> 01:00:17.793
Yeah, I did not use the 400 IM analogy

1455
01:00:17.793 --> 01:00:19.290
when I stopped swimming, for sure.

1456
01:00:19.290 --> 01:00:23.283
Like I didn't turn and acknowledge things and yeah,

1457
01:00:23.283 --> 01:00:25.051
I did not take my own advice,

1458
01:00:25.051 --> 01:00:28.603
but in just the 400 IM situation.

1459
01:00:30.170 --> 01:00:32.330
I was good at acknowledging that, but you're right.

1460
01:00:32.330 --> 01:00:35.170
I think so many things that I did really well

1461
01:00:35.170 --> 01:00:39.442
in my career didn't necessarily translate and benefit

1462
01:00:39.442 --> 01:00:40.840
when something was done.

1463
01:00:40.840 --> 01:00:43.860
Right, so you're on your way though,

1464
01:00:43.860 --> 01:00:47.046
2007 you set your first world record in Melbourne.

1465
01:00:47.046 --> 01:00:50.680
You have your Natalie Coughlin moment, right.

1466
01:00:50.680 --> 01:00:54.827
Seven years after that idea was incepted into your brain.

1467
01:00:54.827 --> 01:00:58.327
And then in 2008, like here we go.

1468
01:00:58.327 --> 01:01:01.429
Like, everything becomes insane.

1469
01:01:01.429 --> 01:01:05.270
Yeah, it, and I could feel it building,

1470
01:01:05.270 --> 01:01:07.490
you know, it's like you're kind of like click, click, click,

1471
01:01:07.490 --> 01:01:09.600
and I hate roller coasters and I've been

1472
01:01:09.600 --> 01:01:12.570
manipulated and forced to be likable.

1473
01:01:12.570 --> 01:01:15.235
But it truly felt like that build up.

1474
01:01:15.235 --> 01:01:16.100
Rationing up.

1475
01:01:16.100 --> 01:01:17.852
Yeah, like 2007, I mean 2007

1476
01:01:17.852 --> 01:01:19.906
is probably one of my favorite years.

1477
01:01:19.906 --> 01:01:23.470
Just because everything, I felt like every time

1478
01:01:23.470 --> 01:01:25.570
I got in the water, it wasn't even like a question

1479
01:01:25.570 --> 01:01:27.420
in my mind, it was just like,

1480
01:01:27.420 --> 01:01:31.260
if I was behind, if I was not feeling great,

1481
01:01:31.260 --> 01:01:32.653
it was like, so.

1482
01:01:32.653 --> 01:01:35.210
Like I just knew, like I had this confidence

1483
01:01:35.210 --> 01:01:36.760
that it was just gonna happen.

1484
01:01:36.760 --> 01:01:41.560
And that, that feeling carried over,

1485
01:01:41.560 --> 01:01:43.610
you know into 2008 leading up.

1486
01:01:43.610 --> 01:01:47.692
But the problem was, it just kept building.

1487
01:01:47.692 --> 01:01:51.023
And I kept, every time I did a great swim or broke a record

1488
01:01:51.023 --> 01:01:53.484
or a restaurant with this, like dinky meat

1489
01:01:53.484 --> 01:01:56.235
and broke the American record in the 1650,

1490
01:01:56.235 --> 01:01:58.770
when it was supposed to just be a training, swim.

1491
01:01:58.770 --> 01:02:00.541
Like it was almost like even your event.

1492
01:02:00.541 --> 01:02:03.810
No, like it just kept, I kept coming going like,

1493
01:02:03.810 --> 01:02:04.700
what is happening?

1494
01:02:04.700 --> 01:02:07.290
Like everything's going well, but.

1495
01:02:07.290 --> 01:02:08.190
Who had that record?

1496
01:02:08.190 --> 01:02:10.263
Was that, was that a Janet Evans record?

1497
01:02:11.805 --> 01:02:14.340
It might've been, I don't know.

1498
01:02:14.340 --> 01:02:15.650
Like I wasn't, I remember like people

1499
01:02:15.650 --> 01:02:17.376
were making fun of me 'cause.

1500
01:02:17.376 --> 01:02:18.597
Now you sound like Caroline.

1501
01:02:18.597 --> 01:02:19.709
I know you're right.

1502
01:02:19.709 --> 01:02:21.008
I thought you would have known that.

1503
01:02:21.008 --> 01:02:22.411
I should have known, I should know.

1504
01:02:22.411 --> 01:02:23.823
'Cause it was the mile I wasn't even,

1505
01:02:25.032 --> 01:02:27.100
yeah, so it was that I almost was kind of

1506
01:02:27.100 --> 01:02:30.056
adding onto the expectation every time I got in

1507
01:02:30.056 --> 01:02:31.830
I'm breaking records and doing this.

1508
01:02:31.830 --> 01:02:34.399
Like each success just adds to the pressure

1509
01:02:34.399 --> 01:02:37.023
and the expectations that ultimately

1510
01:02:37.023 --> 01:02:39.560
you're gonna have to shoulder in Beijing.

1511
01:02:39.560 --> 01:02:43.890
Yeah, and I remember as, you know, six months out,

1512
01:02:43.890 --> 01:02:46.119
three months out you know, more interviews.

1513
01:02:46.119 --> 01:02:49.030
And I remember saying the words

1514
01:02:49.030 --> 01:02:51.750
but inside had kind of freaking out like.

1515
01:02:51.750 --> 01:02:55.380
And not knowing who to admit that to.

1516
01:02:55.380 --> 01:02:57.610
Because I was so like if I admit this,

1517
01:02:57.610 --> 01:03:00.290
like I'm kind of freaking out, like this is a lot,

1518
01:03:00.290 --> 01:03:01.535
like I haven't even won.

1519
01:03:01.535 --> 01:03:03.510
I have any one Olympic medal yet.

1520
01:03:03.510 --> 01:03:06.203
And everyone's swimming a lot of events.

1521
01:03:06.203 --> 01:03:10.240
Same as Michael, and I just remember thinking,

1522
01:03:10.240 --> 01:03:12.860
okay, like just take it one thing at a time.

1523
01:03:12.860 --> 01:03:14.690
At trials I'm summing a ton of events.

1524
01:03:14.690 --> 01:03:18.300
So we're saying even if everything goes wrong,

1525
01:03:18.300 --> 01:03:19.630
I'll at least make it in one event.

1526
01:03:19.630 --> 01:03:21.860
Like that was kind of like my,

1527
01:03:21.860 --> 01:03:22.920
what I'm saying.

1528
01:03:22.920 --> 01:03:24.475
Escape hatch.
Escape, yeah.

1529
01:03:24.475 --> 01:03:25.330
Like it will be okay.

1530
01:03:25.330 --> 01:03:27.730
But meanwhile, you're, you are starting to freak out.

1531
01:03:27.730 --> 01:03:28.563
Yeah.

1532
01:03:28.563 --> 01:03:32.110
And I feel like, I feel like sport has come a long way.

1533
01:03:32.110 --> 01:03:34.763
And even the last decade in terms of reckoning with

1534
01:03:34.763 --> 01:03:39.763
the mental aspects of elite performance and recognizing that

1535
01:03:39.940 --> 01:03:43.280
you know, coaches, parents, therapists,

1536
01:03:43.280 --> 01:03:47.683
whoever need to be you know, checking in more on

1537
01:03:47.683 --> 01:03:49.950
the emotional component here.

1538
01:03:49.950 --> 01:03:52.937
But maybe at that time, it hadn't gotten to that point.

1539
01:03:52.937 --> 01:03:54.547
And it's just like, are you good?

1540
01:03:54.547 --> 01:03:56.224
"Yeah, I'm good."

1541
01:03:56.224 --> 01:03:58.039
Okay, we've talked about that now, right.

1542
01:03:58.039 --> 01:03:58.872
Yeah.

1543
01:03:58.872 --> 01:04:00.990
Without really understanding like, oh man.

1544
01:04:00.990 --> 01:04:03.996
Like what's it like for a 19 year old

1545
01:04:03.996 --> 01:04:07.075
to suddenly be thrust into this position where

1546
01:04:07.075 --> 01:04:10.280
so much attention is being placed you know,

1547
01:04:10.280 --> 01:04:12.718
and expectations are, are being placed on you.

1548
01:04:12.718 --> 01:04:17.718
Yeah, and I think, you know, now talking to even

1549
01:04:17.730 --> 01:04:20.520
my parents you know, my parents were wonderful

1550
01:04:20.520 --> 01:04:21.353
throughout my career.

1551
01:04:21.353 --> 01:04:23.360
They were like, let me in the driver's seat,

1552
01:04:23.360 --> 01:04:24.730
they were supportive.

1553
01:04:24.730 --> 01:04:26.330
They, you know, my dad was the hype man.

1554
01:04:26.330 --> 01:04:28.227
You know, I'd come back from practice.

1555
01:04:28.227 --> 01:04:29.060
Like dad, I hit these paces.

1556
01:04:29.060 --> 01:04:30.250
He used to like "Yes, that's amazing."

1557
01:04:30.250 --> 01:04:33.610
My mom was the more sensible, real it in.

1558
01:04:33.610 --> 01:04:34.920
Like, it hasn't happened yet.

1559
01:04:34.920 --> 01:04:37.203
You know, she was the analytical one.

1560
01:04:38.370 --> 01:04:40.130
But I don't think my mom even says

1561
01:04:40.130 --> 01:04:43.090
now she's like I didn't know how to stop the train

1562
01:04:43.090 --> 01:04:46.040
from going as fast as it was.

1563
01:04:46.040 --> 01:04:48.190
It was just like, go, go, go, go, go.

1564
01:04:48.190 --> 01:04:49.780
But today shows here three months out,

1565
01:04:49.780 --> 01:04:52.014
I'm sitting down with Matt Lauer.

1566
01:04:52.014 --> 01:04:54.090
One-on-one like what was happening?

1567
01:04:54.090 --> 01:04:56.684
And you can see it, I watched that interview with him

1568
01:04:56.684 --> 01:05:00.930
for the first time when I put it in my book.

1569
01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:02.740
Like I'd never seen it.

1570
01:05:02.740 --> 01:05:05.595
And you can, I can just see the,

1571
01:05:05.595 --> 01:05:08.044
I can see what I'm saying where they can see

1572
01:05:08.044 --> 01:05:11.228
the look on my face and you can tell inside I'm like,

1573
01:05:11.228 --> 01:05:13.158
all right, you know.

1574
01:05:13.158 --> 01:05:14.646
Was that before trials or?

1575
01:05:14.646 --> 01:05:15.770
It was before trials.

1576
01:05:15.770 --> 01:05:17.133
Yeah, it was probably,

1577
01:05:17.133 --> 01:05:18.030
I think it was like six before trials.

1578
01:05:18.030 --> 01:05:19.823
He came and you know,

1579
01:05:20.740 --> 01:05:23.110
I just felt like there is the best analogy I always give

1580
01:05:23.110 --> 01:05:25.520
is like there's a barbell on my back, it's 45 pounds.

1581
01:05:25.520 --> 01:05:28.010
I can walk a lot around all day with that on my back.

1582
01:05:28.010 --> 01:05:28.997
But it was just like each day,

1583
01:05:28.997 --> 01:05:31.717
it was like here's that another five other five number five.

1584
01:05:31.717 --> 01:05:35.876
And you know, and I think I put so much emotional energy

1585
01:05:35.876 --> 01:05:38.287
as any athlete does into trials

1586
01:05:38.287 --> 01:05:40.609
because it was every single day,

1587
01:05:40.609 --> 01:05:44.760
you know, a big big events schedule out of the gate.

1588
01:05:44.760 --> 01:05:48.350
And that I remember finishing trials and going,

1589
01:05:48.350 --> 01:05:50.900
oh, I have to do that all over again.

1590
01:05:50.900 --> 01:05:52.017
Right.

1591
01:05:52.017 --> 01:05:52.906
Oh my gosh

1592
01:05:52.906 --> 01:05:55.569
So you go to trials, you qualified for individual events.

1593
01:05:55.569 --> 01:05:57.819
How many of those did you win?

1594
01:05:57.819 --> 01:05:59.477
Did you win all of them?

1595
01:05:59.477 --> 01:06:00.330
Yeah.

1596
01:06:00.330 --> 01:06:02.570
You won, you got for goals,

1597
01:06:02.570 --> 01:06:03.403
for first places at trials.

1598
01:06:03.403 --> 01:06:07.422
200 IM, 400 IM 200 free.

1599
01:06:07.422 --> 01:06:08.740
400 free, 800 free.

1600
01:06:08.740 --> 01:06:11.160
Oh, five event, five right.

1601
01:06:11.160 --> 01:06:12.210
And the only,

1602
01:06:12.210 --> 01:06:15.480
yeah it's like one of those stars aligning moments.

1603
01:06:15.480 --> 01:06:17.120
So those you're, you're no longer

1604
01:06:17.120 --> 01:06:19.373
getting five pound weights added onto that bar.

1605
01:06:19.373 --> 01:06:21.334
Like you're getting yeah, exactly.

1606
01:06:21.334 --> 01:06:24.030
I was just, it's almost like I was doing it to myself.

1607
01:06:24.030 --> 01:06:25.470
I mean, in a good way, right.

1608
01:06:25.470 --> 01:06:27.633
I was swimming out of my mind with

1609
01:06:27.633 --> 01:06:30.660
and so many of the races were close you know.

1610
01:06:30.660 --> 01:06:34.430
The 200 free I think it was seven, eight, one hundreds

1611
01:06:34.430 --> 01:06:37.610
in between me and then there was a super close race

1612
01:06:37.610 --> 01:06:40.240
in the 200 IM between Natalie and Ariana.

1613
01:06:40.240 --> 01:06:44.130
And so, and I'll never forget being so mad

1614
01:06:44.130 --> 01:06:47.236
because I did, the first day to the 400

1615
01:06:47.236 --> 01:06:49.650
the second day I did 400 IM,

1616
01:06:49.650 --> 01:06:50.820
the second day of the 400 free.

1617
01:06:50.820 --> 01:06:54.221
So two days in, I'm already four, four hundreds of racing in

1618
01:06:54.221 --> 01:06:58.600
the next two days, I have six to hundreds between the two.

1619
01:06:58.600 --> 01:07:00.250
Yeah, I think people that aren't familiar

1620
01:07:00.250 --> 01:07:02.368
with swimming don't understand.

1621
01:07:02.368 --> 01:07:04.037
It's not, you're not swimming five times.

1622
01:07:04.037 --> 01:07:06.129
You're swimming like 15 times or something, right.

1623
01:07:06.129 --> 01:07:07.971
Yeah, I think it was 15 times.

1624
01:07:07.971 --> 01:07:09.133
You have to swim you have to

1625
01:07:09.133 --> 01:07:10.420
swim prelim semi's and finals.

1626
01:07:10.420 --> 01:07:12.360
So maybe in the 800, do you do?

1627
01:07:12.360 --> 01:07:13.468
Prelims and finals.

1628
01:07:13.468 --> 01:07:15.225
There's only two for that, right.

1629
01:07:15.225 --> 01:07:16.630
There's only two for the 400 as well.

1630
01:07:16.630 --> 01:07:20.920
It was just, I mean, again, like the US is such a force

1631
01:07:20.920 --> 01:07:23.610
that there was no taking it easy

1632
01:07:23.610 --> 01:07:25.262
or you had to be A game,

1633
01:07:25.262 --> 01:07:27.410
you know when you're going into those people.

1634
01:07:27.410 --> 01:07:30.003
And so yeah, I left the trials,

1635
01:07:30.003 --> 01:07:32.497
I won five, I think Michael won five.

1636
01:07:32.497 --> 01:07:34.280
And the media was like, yes.

1637
01:07:34.280 --> 01:07:35.460
Right here, we have our,

1638
01:07:35.460 --> 01:07:38.470
we have our tightly knit narrative.

1639
01:07:38.470 --> 01:07:41.057
Like you are the female Michael Phelps, right.

1640
01:07:41.057 --> 01:07:44.057
That's a very easy story to tell.

1641
01:07:44.057 --> 01:07:45.560
It's very,

1642
01:07:45.560 --> 01:07:49.724
you know, it's very appealing to the to the public

1643
01:07:49.724 --> 01:07:52.670
because everybody wants to know who the other,

1644
01:07:52.670 --> 01:07:55.524
Michael, the female Michael Phelps is gonna be,

1645
01:07:55.524 --> 01:07:56.980
and you want five, you want five events.

1646
01:07:56.980 --> 01:07:58.580
It's like who else is gonna be, right.

1647
01:07:58.580 --> 01:08:00.054
Yeah, it was fare you know.

1648
01:08:00.054 --> 01:08:01.336
So here we go.

1649
01:08:01.336 --> 01:08:02.544
Yeah.
(Katie laughs)

1650
01:08:02.544 --> 01:08:04.510
It was fair but also if you, you know, obviously.

1651
01:08:04.510 --> 01:08:07.012
But it sets you up, if you don't win as many medals

1652
01:08:07.012 --> 01:08:10.560
as many gold medals as Michael, then you failed.

1653
01:08:10.560 --> 01:08:13.932
Yes, and I will say in my defense,

1654
01:08:13.932 --> 01:08:17.640
you know he was I think he had four world records

1655
01:08:17.640 --> 01:08:18.720
at that point of his, you know.

1656
01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:21.220
And I had one and it was,

1657
01:08:21.220 --> 01:08:22.630
I was trading the world record with

1658
01:08:22.630 --> 01:08:24.970
with another girl, Stephanie Rice.

1659
01:08:24.970 --> 01:08:27.460
And so I didn't feel like,

1660
01:08:27.460 --> 01:08:31.446
oh yeah like I'm going in ranked first and everything.

1661
01:08:31.446 --> 01:08:33.017
You know there were so many other competitors.

1662
01:08:33.017 --> 01:08:34.340
The public doesn't know that.

1663
01:08:34.340 --> 01:08:35.826
Good exactly, the public doesn't know that.

1664
01:08:35.826 --> 01:08:37.031
It doesn't know that.

1665
01:08:37.031 --> 01:08:38.810
Yeah, and we should also point out

1666
01:08:38.810 --> 01:08:41.390
that you're, that Michael was your teammate.

1667
01:08:41.390 --> 01:08:44.495
Like he's swam in NBAC and, and, you know.

1668
01:08:44.495 --> 01:08:47.020
the Olympics at 15, like too easy,

1669
01:08:47.020 --> 01:08:48.528
it was to easy.

1670
01:08:48.528 --> 01:08:50.657
(Katie and Rich laughs)

1671
01:08:50.657 --> 01:08:53.400
Did somebody asks you, like every single day

1672
01:08:53.400 --> 01:08:56.090
what it was like to swim with Michael Phelps?

1673
01:08:56.090 --> 01:08:58.240
Like I get that, it's literally like

1674
01:08:58.240 --> 01:08:59.440
line of questioning goes.

1675
01:08:59.440 --> 01:09:01.680
Oh, you swim, it depends on how curious.

1676
01:09:01.680 --> 01:09:03.297
Like you swim, oh my gosh.

1677
01:09:03.297 --> 01:09:04.557
Do you know Michael Phelps?

1678
01:09:04.557 --> 01:09:05.907
Do you know Michael?

1679
01:09:05.907 --> 01:09:07.283
Or sometimes did you swim?

1680
01:09:07.283 --> 01:09:09.059
And then assuming, you know, Michael Phelps

1681
01:09:09.059 --> 01:09:10.680
or they find out, oh, you swim in the Olympics.

1682
01:09:10.680 --> 01:09:12.890
Like, wait, I'm like, here we go.

1683
01:09:12.890 --> 01:09:14.309
Do you know Michael?

1684
01:09:14.309 --> 01:09:16.005
Yes, and then, so that's definitely, I think.

1685
01:09:16.005 --> 01:09:17.412
So how do you answer that?

1686
01:09:17.412 --> 01:09:18.245
I go yeah.

1687
01:09:18.245 --> 01:09:19.969
It depends on the company I would assume.

1688
01:09:19.969 --> 01:09:22.850
Depends on the company, I usually will just say yes.

1689
01:09:22.850 --> 01:09:25.480
And I always, I mean, Michael's an awesome guy.

1690
01:09:25.480 --> 01:09:28.842
And so I always, you know I think he's been through a lot

1691
01:09:28.842 --> 01:09:30.982
and there's a lot of things

1692
01:09:30.982 --> 01:09:32.690
that have happened in his career.

1693
01:09:32.690 --> 01:09:35.840
Good, and you know, maybe not so not so great.

1694
01:09:35.840 --> 01:09:39.010
And so I always use the opportunity to be like,

1695
01:09:39.010 --> 01:09:42.300
yeah he's an awesome guy and been through a lot.

1696
01:09:42.300 --> 01:09:44.110
And I always, I always liked to use the opportunity

1697
01:09:44.110 --> 01:09:46.530
to do that because sometimes I think it's similarly

1698
01:09:46.530 --> 01:09:48.676
very unfair how you know.

1699
01:09:48.676 --> 01:09:49.871
Can you imagine?

1700
01:09:49.871 --> 01:09:51.370
I mean, yes, you can.

1701
01:09:51.370 --> 01:09:54.790
You're one of the few people who can actually imagine

1702
01:09:54.790 --> 01:09:56.420
what that guy had to endure.

1703
01:09:56.420 --> 01:09:59.610
Of course, you know, there were gonna be moments

1704
01:09:59.610 --> 01:10:02.693
where he was gonna crack under that kind of pressure.

1705
01:10:02.693 --> 01:10:05.500
But what he's done in the wake of all of that

1706
01:10:05.500 --> 01:10:07.900
is so inspiring.

1707
01:10:07.900 --> 01:10:08.950
Very much so.

1708
01:10:08.950 --> 01:10:11.170
Yeah, he and I was

1709
01:10:11.170 --> 01:10:14.100
I was really happy to get to reconnect with him for my book.

1710
01:10:14.100 --> 01:10:15.510
And we were able to talk a little bit

1711
01:10:15.510 --> 01:10:19.023
because my heart feels so much for him

1712
01:10:19.023 --> 01:10:20.540
because like you said, I,

1713
01:10:20.540 --> 01:10:21.960
I can understand, but I can't

1714
01:10:21.960 --> 01:10:23.460
like I can understand a fraction

1715
01:10:23.460 --> 01:10:25.150
of what he feels

1716
01:10:25.150 --> 01:10:27.170
and just having been his teammate,

1717
01:10:27.170 --> 01:10:28.978
you know, in 09 we swam together

1718
01:10:28.978 --> 01:10:30.760
when he was going through a really tough time.

1719
01:10:30.760 --> 01:10:32.830
And so was I, but yeah,

1720
01:10:32.830 --> 01:10:34.090
I just think it's super unfair.

1721
01:10:34.090 --> 01:10:36.380
Sometimes the expectations

1722
01:10:36.380 --> 01:10:38.770
and the you know place we put certain people

1723
01:10:38.770 --> 01:10:41.580
on this pedestal and it's not fair.

1724
01:10:41.580 --> 01:10:42.413
It's just not fair.

1725
01:10:42.413 --> 01:10:43.740
Well, we'd like to put people on that pedestal.

1726
01:10:43.740 --> 01:10:45.120
We like to hoist them up.

1727
01:10:45.120 --> 01:10:47.690
And then we wanna look for how to pull them down

1728
01:10:47.690 --> 01:10:50.387
and any opportunity that we can.

1729
01:10:50.387 --> 01:10:52.530
You know people line up for that.

1730
01:10:52.530 --> 01:10:54.976
Unfortunately that's the way culture works.

1731
01:10:54.976 --> 01:10:55.809
Yeah.

1732
01:10:55.809 --> 01:10:57.910
But then we also like when they come back.

1733
01:10:57.910 --> 01:10:58.750
They but back.

1734
01:10:58.750 --> 01:11:00.290
And he's done that as well.

1735
01:11:00.290 --> 01:11:01.440
So, you know what I mean?

1736
01:11:01.440 --> 01:11:02.900
Yeah.

1737
01:11:02.900 --> 01:11:04.627
All right so,

1738
01:11:04.627 --> 01:11:06.570
you go to Beijing

1739
01:11:06.570 --> 01:11:08.640
with just this unbelievable

1740
01:11:10.090 --> 01:11:14.043
amount of pressure and expectations being placed on you.

1741
01:11:15.300 --> 01:11:17.490
And you talk about this in the book, like

1742
01:11:17.490 --> 01:11:19.483
things don't they don't go to plan.

1743
01:11:20.860 --> 01:11:23.260
You end up walking away with three medals

1744
01:11:23.260 --> 01:11:24.600
out of this experience

1745
01:11:24.600 --> 01:11:28.192
but you don't get any golds and,

1746
01:11:28.192 --> 01:11:30.350
you know you fail to make the final and the 800 free

1747
01:11:30.350 --> 01:11:32.200
like things just didn't

1748
01:11:32.200 --> 01:11:34.817
work out the way that they could have

1749
01:11:34.817 --> 01:11:37.150
and maybe they should have, I don't know.

1750
01:11:37.150 --> 01:11:38.740
Like how do you look back on that

1751
01:11:38.740 --> 01:11:40.710
and think about that time.

1752
01:11:40.710 --> 01:11:42.683
Yeah, I think,

1753
01:11:44.795 --> 01:11:47.670
I think I always wonder, you know, if

1754
01:11:47.670 --> 01:11:49.780
if the 400 IM

1755
01:11:51.300 --> 01:11:53.190
had gone maybe a little better

1756
01:11:53.190 --> 01:11:54.940
like what if I had, what if I had won gold

1757
01:11:54.940 --> 01:11:55.773
in the 400 IM.

1758
01:11:55.773 --> 01:11:57.310
Right, like you got, you got silver

1759
01:11:57.310 --> 01:11:59.520
you were basically touched out.

1760
01:11:59.520 --> 01:12:00.380
The four to three yeah.

1761
01:12:00.380 --> 01:12:01.213
I got touched out

1762
01:12:01.213 --> 01:12:03.343
but the 400 IM actually look at the 400.

1763
01:12:03.343 --> 01:12:05.940
I was one of my best swims of the me

1764
01:12:05.940 --> 01:12:08.730
because yes, I got bronze,

1765
01:12:08.730 --> 01:12:10.430
but I was within a half a second

1766
01:12:10.430 --> 01:12:11.660
of the time I did at trials

1767
01:12:11.660 --> 01:12:13.040
which was the time that

1768
01:12:13.040 --> 01:12:15.050
broke the world record at the time.

1769
01:12:15.050 --> 01:12:16.453
And it was just on that day.

1770
01:12:16.453 --> 01:12:17.286
But those other two girls

1771
01:12:17.286 --> 01:12:20.093
like went under 430 for the first time right.

1772
01:12:20.093 --> 01:12:22.210
And, and I remember in the race thinking

1773
01:12:22.210 --> 01:12:25.556
I must be doing terribly because

1774
01:12:25.556 --> 01:12:28.010
there you know, I can, I can't even see,

1775
01:12:28.010 --> 01:12:30.150
you know she's two seconds ahead of me.

1776
01:12:30.150 --> 01:12:31.730
